program            = 006_many_args_stack_spill
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Variadic calls sized to exhaust every argument register on every target and force stack spill, including an interleaved integer and floating case that spills both register classes
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Header exception: this program includes <stdarg.h>. The corpus sanctions two header
exception classes and only two, and this is the first of them: the programs in area 07 may
include <stdarg.h> and nothing else. The second class is the dedicated bundled-header probe
12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c, which may include the nine required bundled
freestanding headers; it does not apply here, and no program may take both. The exception is
needed because variadic functions cannot be written at all without
va_list, va_start, va_arg and va_end. This program uses exactly those four; va_copy sits
on the same documented row but is not needed here, because no list is copied and none is
traversed twice. stdarg.h is in bcc's own bundled
freestanding set (docs/technical-specifications.md line 208) and is also a freestanding
header supplied by the reference compiler, so it compiles identically under both oracles
and the program remains a single-file reproducer. No other header is included; in
particular stdio.h is not included, because bcc ships none. printf is hand-declared.
Argument counts match retrieval counts exactly, which is the critical precondition here
because the calls are large. The int group declares count 20 and supplies exactly 20 int
arguments. The long long group declares count 12 and supplies exactly 12 long long
arguments, each written with the LL suffix. The double group declares count 16 and
supplies exactly 16 double arguments. The interleaved group declares pairs 10 and supplies
exactly 20 arguments, strictly alternating int then double and beginning with an int, and
the consumer retrieves an int then a double in that same order in each of its 10
iterations. No va_list is ever read past the number of arguments actually supplied.
Variadic discipline: va_start names count in the three homogeneous helpers and pairs in
the interleaved helper, in each case the last named parameter. Each va_start has exactly
one matching va_end in the same function. There is no va_copy and no va_list forwarding in
this program.
Retrieval types: int, long long and double are not subject to the default argument
promotions, so va_arg(ap, int), va_arg(ap, long long) and va_arg(ap, double) retrieve
exactly the types supplied. va_arg(ap, float) and va_arg(ap, long) never appear.
Arithmetic: no signed overflow is possible anywhere. The int group is accumulated into a
long long, which is a widening same-signedness conversion needing no cast, and its totals
are 20190 and 40190. The long long totals are 12000000000066 and 36000000000066, far
inside the 64-bit range. Floating addition is on dyadic rationals only. No int is added to
a double accumulator or the reverse. Every implicit conversion that -Wconversion could
flag is written as an explicit cast, specifically (long long)k in the long long generator
and (double)k and (double)(k + 1) in the two floating generators. There is no shift, no
division, no aliasing violation, no object modified twice between sequence points, and no
dependence on padding or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
No uninitialized reads: every element of every volatile source array is written by a first
loop before a second loop reads it back into plain storage, and every array index stays
inside its declared bound of 20, 12, 16 or 10.
Evaluation order: main stores each returning helper's result in a named temporary before
printing it, so no call has more than one side-effecting argument. In the runtime variants
the volatile source arrays are written and then read back into plain arrays before the
call, so the twenty arguments in the call expression are pure reads of a plain array and
the unspecified argument-evaluation order is unobservable. This is also what prevents an
optimizer from constant-propagating the arguments and hiding a spill-slot addressing
defect, which is the whole reason the runtime variant exists.
Warning gate: this program carries no ub_audit_flags key, and that absence is a positive
statement rather than an omission. It passes the full default gate
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror unmodified, which
was verified by running that gate over this source, and area 07 sanctions no deviation.
The key exists only to record a deviation, so restating the full gate would claim a
narrowing that was never made and would demand a recorded reason for it.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Why these argument counts, stated per target rather than as a single universal model. The
repository documents each backend's argument-register inventory and nothing beyond it, so the
reasoning below is confined to those inventories plus the counts this program actually passes.
x86-64 System V AMD64 (docs/technical-specifications.md line 546) classifies each argument
INTEGER, SSE or MEMORY and lists six integer argument registers rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8 and r9
together with eight SSE argument registers xmm0 to xmm7. i686 cdecl (line 553) documents no
argument registers at all: every argument is pushed on the stack, right to left, with caller
cleanup, so on i686 the stack is the only path and there is no register file to exhaust.
AArch64 AAPCS64 (line 559) documents eight integer argument registers x0 to x7 and eight SIMD
and floating-point argument registers v0 to v7. RISC-V 64 LP64D (line 565) documents eight
integer argument registers a0 to a7 and eight floating-point argument registers fa0 to fa7.
Across the three register-passing targets the largest documented integer inventory is
therefore eight and the largest documented floating inventory is eight. Each helper declares
two named parameters ahead of its ellipsis, and each group passes more variadic arguments of
a single class than any target documents registers for: twenty int arguments, twelve long
long arguments, sixteen double arguments, and ten interleaved int and double pairs, which is
ten of each class in one call. On every register-passing target at least one argument of the
class under test must therefore reach the callee through memory, and on i686 all of them do -
the sharpest contrast in the matrix, and the reason i686 is kept in the target list rather
than dismissed as uninteresting.
The interleaved group is the decisive case, because it overflows both classes in the same
call: ten integers against a documented maximum of eight, and ten floating values against a
documented maximum of eight, on all three register-passing targets simultaneously. That is
the condition under which a variadic argument-area defect is most likely to surface.
How each argument is actually routed - which register, which stack slot, in what order, and
what additional bookkeeping a particular target's variadic sequence may require - is the
ABI's decision and each backend's implementation detail. This record makes no claim about it,
for two reasons: the repository documents register inventories rather than variadic routing
rules, so any such claim would exceed its cited basis; and the suite is a black-box oracle in
which only the retrieved values reach stdout. A correct backend agrees with the other three
however it routes the arguments, and one line per retrieved argument means a divergent line
names the exact argument slot that was mishandled without this record having to predict where
that slot lives.
Width normalization: the types long and unsigned long are never used, because sizeof(long)
and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64
(docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457 to 462). long long is 64 bits on all four targets
and is used in its place. Only int, long long and double appear, and the printf
conversions are limited to %s, %d, %lld and %.6f. No address, no pointer value, no sizeof
result and no plain char value is printed.
Floating determinism: every floating value is a multiple of 0.25 or 0.5 and is therefore a
dyadic rational exactly representable in IEEE binary64, and all floating output is printed
at fixed precision with %.6f, so the rendered text is byte-identical on all four backends.
Line count and structure of the golden record: twenty int lines plus one sum, twenty
runtime int lines plus one sum, twelve long long lines plus one sum, twelve runtime long
long lines plus one sum, sixteen double lines plus one sum, sixteen runtime double lines
plus one sum, ten interleaved lines plus two sums, and ten runtime interleaved lines plus
two sums, for exactly 126 lines. One line per retrieved argument or pair is what makes a
spill defect diagnosable rather than merely detectable, because a single divergent line
identifies the exact argument slot the backend mishandled.
Cross-backend comparability: integer and double variadic argument passing was measured to
produce byte-identical results on all four targets at all three optimization levels, so no
target is restricted and all three oracles are enabled. Any cross-backend difference in
this program is a genuine ABI-level finding rather than a documented implementation-defined
difference, and it must be reported as a finding rather than patched: a verbatim reproducer,
a byte-for-byte copy of this program, together with the captured per-compiler and per-backend
outputs, an environment fingerprint, its recorded minimization status and the exact
reproduction commands. Reduction is never
performed during a run; it is a supervised activity carried out on the copy before a finding
is promoted to the curated set.
END
